Harbor: overlay windows stay out of the fullscreen Space, and a fullscreen program no longer takes its Desktop
back from an app the user chose (#82).

On top of 0011 (a fullscreen program gets a macOS fullscreen Space of its own) and 0009 (a fullscreen program
gets its Desktop back after a switch of Desktops).

1. 0011 moved every visible, unowned top-level window that covers a screen into a Space of its own. That is right
   for a program's own window, and wrong for a window laid over the Desktop: a screen dimmer, a screenshot
   region picker, a transparent overlay ended up alone on an empty Space. Left out now (-isOverlayWindow):
     - WS_EX_LAYERED windows (an alpha value, a colour key, per-pixel alpha), WS_EX_TRANSPARENT (click-through),
       WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW and WS_EX_NOACTIVATE windows, and windows cut to a region (SetWindowRgn).
   The window state passed to Cocoa gets one more bit for them, no_own_space: winemac tracked none of the styles
   for a window without a title bar (it has no ignoresMouseEvents handling at all), and what the Cocoa window
   does know, its alpha value, per-pixel alpha and shape image, arrives only after the window is shown
   (UpdateLayeredWindow shows the window first; a region's or colour key's shape comes with the first drawing),
   which is after the decision. A shape that is already there counts as well. A layered window that is in fact
   opaque is left out too; it keeps Wine's raised fullscreen window.
   WS_EX_TOPMOST ("floating") windows are deliberately not left out: Direct3D's exclusive fullscreen makes the
   game's window topmost, and those are the windows 0011 is for.
   The check is made when the window would enter its Space; a window that turns into an overlay while it is in
   one stays there.

2. 0009's "left by a switch of Desktops" mark never expired. With the Desktop of a (Wine-raised) fullscreen
   window out of view, picking another app whose window is on that Desktop brought the Desktop back, and the
   fullscreen program then took activation away from the app just picked, however long ago the switch was. The
   mark is now dropped when another app is activated later than 2 seconds after the program lost activation
   (the activation macOS itself makes as part of the switch falls inside those 2 seconds, the same allowance
   0009 already uses). Chosen over a timeout: a timeout either still overrules a choice made within it or stops
   giving the Desktop back after a long look at another one, and it is no less code.
   Cost: after a switch away, using a second app there and switching back, the fullscreen program is no longer
   brought to the front by this; macOS activates whatever is frontmost on that Desktop.

Not changed: the review's third point, that a program going windowed while macOS still animates its window into
the Space stays there because -windowDidEnterFullScreen: doesn't look again, did not reproduce (delays of 50 to
1500 ms after going fullscreen: the window was back on the Desktop within 3 seconds every time; Wine sets the
frame again after the animation, which leaves the Space through -updateFullscreen).

Tests: WindowsTests/graphics/spaces.sh; WindowsTests/graphics/fullscreen.sh for 0011 itself.

